{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-328531" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Solve the inequality\n$\\frac{2x+7}{|x|+{x}^{2}+1}\\ge \\frac{x+4}{|x|+{x}^{2}+1}.$\n\n$x$\n{{field-1}}{{field-2}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981762/state.json" "file_sha256" "b32f01ca9cf74dcc6044655235d91ad3709a79f147029d5bf330be973fdd475e"} {"question" "q-328531" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We know that the denominator $|x|+{x}^{2}+1$ is positive for all values of $x.$ So, we can multiply both sides of the inequality by $|x|+{x}^{2}+1$ and solve the resulting inequality as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2x+7}{|x|+{x}^{2}+1} & \\ge \\frac{x+4}{|x|+{x}^{2}+1} \\\\ (|x|+{x}^{2}+1)\\cdot \\frac{2x+7}{|x|+{x}^{2}+1} & \\ge (|x|+{x}^{2}+1)\\cdot \\frac{x+4}{|x|+{x}^{2}+1} \\\\ 2x+7 & \\ge x+4 \\\\ x & \\ge -3 \\\\ x & \\ge \\,-3\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981762/state.json" "file_sha256" "b32f01ca9cf74dcc6044655235d91ad3709a79f147029d5bf330be973fdd475e"} {"question" "q-328531" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981762/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "7994e56f5fb1194063d808625e3816e095444e38e43c82b7f99341e9ac4cb746"} {"question" "q-328531" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981762/state.json" "file_sha256" "b32f01ca9cf74dcc6044655235d91ad3709a79f147029d5bf330be973fdd475e"} {"question" "q-328531" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\ge"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981762/state.json" "file_sha256" "b32f01ca9cf74dcc6044655235d91ad3709a79f147029d5bf330be973fdd475e"} {"question" "q-328531" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981762/state.json" "file_sha256" "b32f01ca9cf74dcc6044655235d91ad3709a79f147029d5bf330be973fdd475e"} {"question" "q-328531" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-3"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981762/state.json" "file_sha256" "b32f01ca9cf74dcc6044655235d91ad3709a79f147029d5bf330be973fdd475e"} {"question" "q-89651" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Solve the inequality\n\n$$\n\\frac{2{x}^{4}+3x}{{x}^{4}+2}\\le 2.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981762/state.json" "file_sha256" "b32f01ca9cf74dcc6044655235d91ad3709a79f147029d5bf330be973fdd475e"} {"question" "q-89651" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We know that the denominator ${x}^{4}+2$ is positive for all values of $x.$ So, we can multiply both sides of the inequality by ${x}^{4}+2$ and solve the resulting inequality as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2{x}^{4}+3x}{{x}^{4}+2} & \\le 2 \\\\ ({x}^{4}+2)\\cdot \\frac{2{x}^{4}+3x}{{x}^{4}+2} & \\le ({x}^{4}+2)\\cdot 2 \\\\ 2{x}^{4}+3x & \\le 2{x}^{4}+4 \\\\ 3x & \\le 4 \\\\ x & \\le \\frac{4}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981762/state.json" "file_sha256" "b32f01ca9cf74dcc6044655235d91ad3709a79f147029d5bf330be973fdd475e"} {"question" "q-89651" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981762/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "7994e56f5fb1194063d808625e3816e095444e38e43c82b7f99341e9ac4cb746"} {"question" "q-89651" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981762/state.json" "file_sha256" "b32f01ca9cf74dcc6044655235d91ad3709a79f147029d5bf330be973fdd475e"} {"question" "q-89651" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "x\\ge 1"] ["math" "x\\ge \\frac{3}{2}"] ["math" "x\\le 3"] ["math" "x\\le 4"] ["math" "x\\le \\frac{4}{3}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981762/state.json" "file_sha256" "b32f01ca9cf74dcc6044655235d91ad3709a79f147029d5bf330be973fdd475e"} {"question" "q-89651" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "x\\le \\frac{4}{3}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981762/state.json" "file_sha256" "b32f01ca9cf74dcc6044655235d91ad3709a79f147029d5bf330be973fdd475e"} {"question" "q-328412" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Solve the inequality $\\frac{9-2x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+3}>\\frac{x-1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+3}.$\n\n$x$\n{{field-1}}{{field-2}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981762/state.json" "file_sha256" "b32f01ca9cf74dcc6044655235d91ad3709a79f147029d5bf330be973fdd475e"} {"question" "q-328412" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Note that the denominator $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+3$ is always positive. We know this because\n\n$$\n-1\\le \\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\le 1\\,⇒\\,2\\le \\operatorname{sin}⁡x+3\\le 4.\n$$\n\nSo, we can multiply both sides of the inequality by $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+3$ and solve the resulting inequality as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{9-2x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+3} & >\\frac{x-1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+3} \\\\ (\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+3)\\cdot \\frac{9-2x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+3} & >(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+3)\\cdot \\frac{x-1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+3} \\\\ 9-2x & >x-1 \\\\ -3x & >-10 \\\\ x & <\\,\\frac{10}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981762/state.json" "file_sha256" "b32f01ca9cf74dcc6044655235d91ad3709a79f147029d5bf330be973fdd475e"} {"question" "q-328412" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981762/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "7994e56f5fb1194063d808625e3816e095444e38e43c82b7f99341e9ac4cb746"} {"question" "q-328412" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981762/state.json" "file_sha256" "b32f01ca9cf74dcc6044655235d91ad3709a79f147029d5bf330be973fdd475e"} {"question" "q-328412" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "<"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981762/state.json" "file_sha256" "b32f01ca9cf74dcc6044655235d91ad3709a79f147029d5bf330be973fdd475e"} {"question" "q-328412" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981762/state.json" "file_sha256" "b32f01ca9cf74dcc6044655235d91ad3709a79f147029d5bf330be973fdd475e"} {"question" "q-328412" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{10}{3}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981762/state.json" "file_sha256" "b32f01ca9cf74dcc6044655235d91ad3709a79f147029d5bf330be973fdd475e"} {"question" "q-328530" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Solve the inequality\n$\\frac{3x+4}{ln\\,({x}^{2}+1)+{e}^{x}}>\\frac{x+10}{ln\\,({x}^{2}+1)+{e}^{x}}.$\n\n$x$\n{{field-1}}{{field-2}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981762/state.json" "file_sha256" "b32f01ca9cf74dcc6044655235d91ad3709a79f147029d5bf330be973fdd475e"} {"question" "q-328530" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We know that the denominator $\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+1)+{e}^{x}$ is positive for all values of $x.$ So, we can multiply both sides of the inequality by $\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+1)+{e}^{x}$ and solve the resulting inequality as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3x+4}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+1)+{e}^{x}} & >\\frac{x+10}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+1)+{e}^{x}} \\\\ (\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+1)+{e}^{x})\\cdot \\frac{3x+4}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+1)+{e}^{x}} & >(\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+1)+{e}^{x})\\cdot \\frac{x+10}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+1)+{e}^{x}} \\\\ 3x+4 & >x+10 \\\\ 2x & >6 \\\\ x & >\\,3\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981762/state.json" "file_sha256" "b32f01ca9cf74dcc6044655235d91ad3709a79f147029d5bf330be973fdd475e"} {"question" "q-328530" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981762/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "7994e56f5fb1194063d808625e3816e095444e38e43c82b7f99341e9ac4cb746"} {"question" "q-328530" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981762/state.json" "file_sha256" "b32f01ca9cf74dcc6044655235d91ad3709a79f147029d5bf330be973fdd475e"} {"question" "q-328530" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math ">"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981762/state.json" "file_sha256" "b32f01ca9cf74dcc6044655235d91ad3709a79f147029d5bf330be973fdd475e"} {"question" "q-328530" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981762/state.json" "file_sha256" "b32f01ca9cf74dcc6044655235d91ad3709a79f147029d5bf330be973fdd475e"} {"question" "q-328530" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "3"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981762/state.json" "file_sha256" "b32f01ca9cf74dcc6044655235d91ad3709a79f147029d5bf330be973fdd475e"} {"question" "q-328407" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Solve the inequality $\\frac{4{y}^{3}+11y-5}{{y}^{2}+2}\\le 4y.$\n\n$y$\n{{field-1}}{{field-2}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981762/state.json" "file_sha256" "b32f01ca9cf74dcc6044655235d91ad3709a79f147029d5bf330be973fdd475e"} {"question" "q-328407" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We know that the denominator ${y}^{2}+2$ is positive for all values of $y.$ So, we can multiply both sides of the inequality by ${y}^{2}+2$ and solve the resulting inequality as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4{y}^{3}+11y-5}{{y}^{2}+2} & \\le 4y \\\\ ({y}^{2}+2)\\cdot \\frac{4{y}^{3}+11y-5}{{y}^{2}+2} & \\le ({y}^{2}+2)\\cdot 4y \\\\ 4{y}^{3}+11y-5 & \\le 4{y}^{3}+8y \\\\ 11y-5 & \\le 8y \\\\ 3y-5 & \\le 0 \\\\ y & \\le \\,\\frac{5}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981762/state.json" "file_sha256" "b32f01ca9cf74dcc6044655235d91ad3709a79f147029d5bf330be973fdd475e"} {"question" "q-328407" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981762/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "7994e56f5fb1194063d808625e3816e095444e38e43c82b7f99341e9ac4cb746"} {"question" "q-328407" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981762/state.json" "file_sha256" "b32f01ca9cf74dcc6044655235d91ad3709a79f147029d5bf330be973fdd475e"} {"question" "q-328407" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\le"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981762/state.json" "file_sha256" "b32f01ca9cf74dcc6044655235d91ad3709a79f147029d5bf330be973fdd475e"} {"question" "q-328407" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981762/state.json" "file_sha256" "b32f01ca9cf74dcc6044655235d91ad3709a79f147029d5bf330be973fdd475e"} {"question" "q-328407" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{5}{3}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981762/state.json" "file_sha256" "b32f01ca9cf74dcc6044655235d91ad3709a79f147029d5bf330be973fdd475e"} {"question" "q-328553" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Solve the inequality\n$({e}^{2x}+{x}^{4})(5x-1)<0.$\n\n$x$\n{{field-1}}{{field-2}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981762/state.json" "file_sha256" "b32f01ca9cf74dcc6044655235d91ad3709a79f147029d5bf330be973fdd475e"} {"question" "q-328553" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Since ${e}^{2x}+{x}^{4}$ is always positive, we can divide both sides of the inequality by this quantity. By doing this, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{({e}^{2x}+{x}^{4})(5x-1)}{{e}^{2x}+{x}^{4}} & <\\frac{0}{{e}^{2x}+{x}^{4}} \\\\ 5x-1 & <0 \\\\ 5x & <1 \\\\ x & <\\,\\frac{1}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981762/state.json" "file_sha256" "b32f01ca9cf74dcc6044655235d91ad3709a79f147029d5bf330be973fdd475e"} {"question" "q-328553" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981762/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "7994e56f5fb1194063d808625e3816e095444e38e43c82b7f99341e9ac4cb746"} {"question" "q-328553" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981762/state.json" "file_sha256" "b32f01ca9cf74dcc6044655235d91ad3709a79f147029d5bf330be973fdd475e"} {"question" "q-328553" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "<"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981762/state.json" "file_sha256" "b32f01ca9cf74dcc6044655235d91ad3709a79f147029d5bf330be973fdd475e"} {"question" "q-328553" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/t