{"question" "q-328549" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981762/state.json" "file_sha256" "b32f01ca9cf74dcc6044655235d91ad3709a79f147029d5bf330be973fdd475e"} {"question" "q-328549" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math ">"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981762/state.json" "file_sha256" "b32f01ca9cf74dcc6044655235d91ad3709a79f147029d5bf330be973fdd475e"} {"question" "q-328549" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981762/state.json" "file_sha256" "b32f01ca9cf74dcc6044655235d91ad3709a79f147029d5bf330be973fdd475e"} {"question" "q-328549" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-\\frac{5}{7}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981762/state.json" "file_sha256" "b32f01ca9cf74dcc6044655235d91ad3709a79f147029d5bf330be973fdd475e"} {"question" "q-89658" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Solve the inequality\n\n$$\n4x{e}^{2x}-5{e}^{2x}\\le 0.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981762/state.json" "file_sha256" "b32f01ca9cf74dcc6044655235d91ad3709a79f147029d5bf330be973fdd475e"} {"question" "q-89658" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we factor the given inequality, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4x{e}^{2x}-5{e}^{2x} & \\le 0 \\\\ {e}^{2x}(4x-5) & \\le 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince ${e}^{2x}$ is always positive, we can divide both sides of the inequality by this quantity. By doing this, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{e}^{2x}(4x-5)}{{e}^{2x}} & \\le \\frac{0}{{e}^{2x}} \\\\ 4x-5 & \\le 0 \\\\ 4x & \\le 5 \\\\ x & \\le \\frac{5}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981762/state.json" "file_sha256" "b32f01ca9cf74dcc6044655235d91ad3709a79f147029d5bf330be973fdd475e"} {"question" "q-89658" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981762/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "7994e56f5fb1194063d808625e3816e095444e38e43c82b7f99341e9ac4cb746"} {"question" "q-89658" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981762/state.json" "file_sha256" "b32f01ca9cf74dcc6044655235d91ad3709a79f147029d5bf330be973fdd475e"} {"question" "q-89658" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "x\\ge 0"] ["math" "x\\ge \\frac{2}{5}"] ["math" "x\\le 4"] ["math" "x\\le 5"] ["math" "x\\le \\frac{5}{4}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981762/state.json" "file_sha256" "b32f01ca9cf74dcc6044655235d91ad3709a79f147029d5bf330be973fdd475e"} {"question" "q-89658" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "x\\le \\frac{5}{4}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981762/state.json" "file_sha256" "b32f01ca9cf74dcc6044655235d91ad3709a79f147029d5bf330be973fdd475e"} {"question" "q-89661" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Solve the inequality\n\n$$\n6{x}^{5}+18x>3x.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981762/state.json" "file_sha256" "b32f01ca9cf74dcc6044655235d91ad3709a79f147029d5bf330be973fdd475e"} {"question" "q-89661" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we move all terms to the left-hand side and factor:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6{x}^{5}+18x & >3x \\\\ 6{x}^{5}+15x & >0 \\\\ 3x(2{x}^{4}+5) & >0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $2{x}^{4}+5$ is always positive, we can divide both sides of the inequality by this quantity. By doing this, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3x(2{x}^{4}+5)}{2{x}^{4}+5} & >\\frac{0}{2{x}^{4}+5} \\\\ 3x & >0 \\\\ x & >0.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981762/state.json" "file_sha256" "b32f01ca9cf74dcc6044655235d91ad3709a79f147029d5bf330be973fdd475e"} {"question" "q-89661" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981762/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "7994e56f5fb1194063d808625e3816e095444e38e43c82b7f99341e9ac4cb746"} {"question" "q-89661" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981762/state.json" "file_sha256" "b32f01ca9cf74dcc6044655235d91ad3709a79f147029d5bf330be973fdd475e"} {"question" "q-89661" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "x<1"] ["math" "x<5"] ["math" "x>0"] ["math" "x>2"] ["math" "x>4"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981762/state.json" "file_sha256" "b32f01ca9cf74dcc6044655235d91ad3709a79f147029d5bf330be973fdd475e"} {"question" "q-89661" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "x>0"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981762/state.json" "file_sha256" "b32f01ca9cf74dcc6044655235d91ad3709a79f147029d5bf330be973fdd475e"} {"question" "q-89562" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Solve the inequality\n\n$$\n(3{cos}^{2}⁡x+4)(x-1)\\le 0.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981762/state.json" "file_sha256" "b32f01ca9cf74dcc6044655235d91ad3709a79f147029d5bf330be973fdd475e"} {"question" "q-89562" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Note that the factor $3{cos}^{2}⁡x+4$ is always positive. We know this because\n\n$$\n0\\le {cos}^{2}⁡x\\le 1\\,⇒\\,4\\le 3{cos}^{2}⁡x+4\\le 7.\n$$\n\nSince $3{cos}^{2}⁡x+4$ is always positive, we can divide both sides of the inequality by this quantity. By doing this, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(3{cos}^{2}⁡x+4)(x-1)}{3{cos}^{2}⁡x+4} & \\le \\frac{0}{3{cos}^{2}⁡x+4} \\\\ x-1 & \\le 0 \\\\ x & \\le 1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981762/state.json" "file_sha256" "b32f01ca9cf74dcc6044655235d91ad3709a79f147029d5bf330be973fdd475e"} {"question" "q-89562" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981762/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "7994e56f5fb1194063d808625e3816e095444e38e43c82b7f99341e9ac4cb746"} {"question" "q-89562" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981762/state.json" "file_sha256" "b32f01ca9cf74dcc6044655235d91ad3709a79f147029d5bf330be973fdd475e"} {"question" "q-89562" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "x\\ge -1"] ["math" "x\\ge 0"] ["math" "x\\ge 2"] ["math" "x\\le 0"] ["math" "x\\le 1"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981762/state.json" "file_sha256" "b32f01ca9cf74dcc6044655235d91ad3709a79f147029d5bf330be973fdd475e"} {"question" "q-89562" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "x\\le 1"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981762/state.json" "file_sha256" "b32f01ca9cf74dcc6044655235d91ad3709a79f147029d5bf330be973fdd475e"} {"question" "q-116263" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Solve the inequality\n\n$$\n(-1-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(|x|+2))(4x+7)\\le 0.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981762/state.json" "file_sha256" "b32f01ca9cf74dcc6044655235d91ad3709a79f147029d5bf330be973fdd475e"} {"question" "q-116263" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Since $-1-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(|x|+2)$ is always negative, we can divide both sides of the inequality by this quantity, but we have to remember to flip the sign of the inequality. By doing this, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-1-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(|x|+2))(4x+7) & \\le 0 \\\\ \\frac{(-1-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(|x|+2))(4x+7)}{(-1-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(|x|+2))} & \\ge \\frac{0}{(-1-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(|x|+2))} \\\\ 4x+7 & \\ge 0 \\\\ 4x & \\ge -7 \\\\ x & \\ge -\\frac{7}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981762/state.json" "file_sha256" "b32f01ca9cf74dcc6044655235d91ad3709a79f147029d5bf330be973fdd475e"} {"question" "q-116263" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981762/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "7994e56f5fb1194063d808625e3816e095444e38e43c82b7f99341e9ac4cb746"} {"question" "q-116263" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981762/state.json" "file_sha256" "b32f01ca9cf74dcc6044655235d91ad3709a79f147029d5bf330be973fdd475e"} {"question" "q-116263" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "x\\ge -\\frac{7}{4}"] ["math" "x\\ge \\frac{1}{2}"] ["math" "x\\ge \\frac{3}{4}"] ["math" "x\\le -\\frac{1}{2}"] ["math" "x\\le \\frac{7}{4}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981762/state.json" "file_sha256" "b32f01ca9cf74dcc6044655235d91ad3709a79f147029d5bf330be973fdd475e"} {"question" "q-116263" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "x\\ge -\\frac{7}{4}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981762/state.json" "file_sha256" "b32f01ca9cf74dcc6044655235d91ad3709a79f147029d5bf330be973fdd475e"} {"question" "q-89676" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Solve the inequality\n\n$$\n(-3{x}^{2}-4)(x-1)<0.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981762/state.json" "file_sha256" "b32f01ca9cf74dcc6044655235d91ad3709a79f147029d5bf330be973fdd475e"} {"question" "q-89676" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Since $-3{x}^{2}-4$ is always negative, we can divide both sides of the inequality by this quantity, but we have to remember to flip the sign of the inequality. By doing this, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-3{x}^{2}-4)(x-1) & <0 \\\\ \\frac{(-3{x}^{2}-4)(x-1)}{-3{x}^{2}-4} & >\\frac{0}{-3{x}^{2}-4} \\\\ x-1 & >0 \\\\ x & >1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981762/state.json" "file_sha256" "b32f01ca9cf74dcc6044655235d91ad3709a79f147029d5bf330be973fdd475e"} {"question" "q-89676" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981762/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "7994e56f5fb1194063d808625e3816e095444e38e43c82b7f99341e9ac4cb746"} {"question" "q-89676" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981762/state.json" "file_sha256" "b32f01ca9cf74dcc6044655235d91ad3709a79f147029d5bf330be973fdd475e"} {"question" "q-89676" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "x<-1"] ["math" "x<2"] ["math" "x>-2"] ["math" "x>0"] ["math" "x>1"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981762/state.json" "file_sha256" "b32f01ca9cf74dcc6044655235d91ad3709a79f147029d5bf330be973fdd475e"} {"question" "q-89676" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "x>1"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981762/state.json" "file_sha256" "b32f01ca9cf74dcc6044655235d91ad3709a79f147029d5bf330be973fdd475e"} {"question" "q-116265" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Solve the inequality\n\n$$\n(2\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x+3)-3\\pi )(2x+9)<0.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981762/state.json" "file_sha256" "b32f01ca9cf74dcc6044655235d91ad3709a79f147029d5bf330be973fdd475e"} {"question" "q-116265" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Note that the factor $2\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x+3)-3\\pi $ is always negative. We know this because\n\n$$\n-\\frac{\\pi }{2}<\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x+3)<\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\,⇒\\,-4\\pi <2\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x+3)-3\\pi <-2\\pi .\n$$\n\nSince $2\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x+3)-3\\pi $ is always negative, we can divide both sides of the inequality by this quantity, but we have to remember to flip the sign of the inequality. By doing this, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(2\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x+3)-3\\pi )(2x+9) & <0 \\\\ \\frac{(2\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x+3)-3\\pi )(2x+9)}{2\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x+3)-3\\pi } & >\\frac{0}{2\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x+3)-3\\pi } \\\\ 2x+9 & >0 \\\\ 2x & >-9 \\\\ x & >-\\frac{9}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981762/state.json" "file_sha256" "b32f01ca9cf74dcc6044655235d91ad3709a79f147029d5bf330be973fdd475e"} {"question" "q-116265" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981762/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "7994e56f5fb1194063d808625e3816e095444e38e43c82b7f99341e9ac4cb746"} {"question" "q-116265" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981762/state.json" "file_sha256" "b32f01ca9cf74dcc6044655235d91ad3709a79f147029d5bf330be973fdd475e"} {"question" "q-116265" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "x<-\\frac{3}{2}"] ["math" "x<\\frac{9}{2}"] ["math" "x>-2"] ["math" "x>-\\frac{9}{2}"] ["math" "x>\\frac{3}{2}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981762/state.json" "file_sha256" "b32f01ca9cf74dcc6044655235d91ad3709a79f147029d5bf330be973fdd475e"} {"question" "q-116265" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "x>-\\frac{9}{2}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981762/state.json" "file_sha256" "b32f01ca9cf74dcc6044655235d91ad3709a79f147029d5bf330be973fdd475e"} {"question" "q-116269" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Solve the inequality\n\n$$\n\\frac{2x+3}{\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x)-\\pi }\\ge \\frac{2-7x}{\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x)-\\pi }.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981762/state.json" "file_sha256" "b32f01ca9cf74dcc6044655235d91ad3709a79f147029d5bf330be973fdd475e"} {"question" "q-116269" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Note that the factor $\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x)-\\pi $ is always negative. We know this because\n\n$$\n-\\frac{\\pi }{2}<\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x<\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\,⇒\\,-\\frac{3\\pi }{2}<\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x)-\\pi <-\\frac{\\pi }{2}.\n$$\n\nSince $\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x)-\\pi $ is always negative, we can multiply both sides of the inequality by this quantity, but we have to remember to flip the sign of the inequality. By doing this, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2x+3}{\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x)-\\pi } & \\ge \\frac{2-7x}{\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x)-\\pi } \\\\ (\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x)-\\pi )\\cdot \\frac{2x+3}{\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x)-\\pi } & \\le (\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x)-\\pi )\\cdot \\frac{2-7x}{\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x)-\\pi } \\\\ 2x+3 & \\le 2-7x \\\\ 9x & \\le -1 \\\\ x & \\le -\\frac{1}{9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981762/state.json" "file_sha256" "b32f01ca9cf74dcc6044655235d91ad3709a79f147029d5bf330be973fdd475e"} {"question" "q-116269" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981762/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "7994e56f5fb1194063d808625e3816e095444e38e43c82b7f99341e9ac4cb746"} {"question" "q-116269" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981762/state.json" "file_sha256" "b32f01ca9cf74dcc6044655235d91ad3709a79f147029d5bf330be973fdd475e"} {"question" "q-116269" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "x\\ge -\\frac{1}{3}"] ["math" "x\\ge \\frac{1}{9}"] ["math" "x\\le -\\frac{1}{9}"] ["math" "x\\le -\\frac{2}{3}"] ["math" "x\\le \\frac{1}{3}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981762/state.json" "file_sha256" "b32f01ca9cf74dcc6044655235d91ad3709a79f147029d5bf330be973fdd475e"} {"question" "q-116269" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "x\\le -\\frac{1}{9}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981762/state.json" "file_sha256" "b32f01ca9cf74dcc6044655235d91ad3709a79f147029d5bf330be973fdd475e"} {"question" "q-89671" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Solve the inequality\n\n$$\n\\frac{x+2}{2{sin}^{2}⁡x-3}>\\frac{4-3x}{2{sin}^{2}⁡x-3}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981762/state.json" "file_sha256" "b32f01ca9cf74dcc6044655235d91ad3709a79f147029d5bf330be973fdd475e"} {"question" "q-89671" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Note that the factor $2{sin}^{2}⁡x-3$ is always negative. We know this because\n\n$$\n0\\le {sin}^{2}⁡x\\le 1\\,⇒\\,-3\\le 2{sin}^{2}⁡x-3\\le -1.\n$$\n\nSince $2{sin}^{2}⁡x-3$ is always negative, we can multiply both sides of the inequality by this quantity, but we have to remember to flip the sign of the inequality. By doing this, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x+2}{2{sin}^{2}⁡x-3} & >\\frac{4-3x}{2{sin}^{2}⁡x-3} \\\\ (2{sin}^{2}⁡x-3)\\cdot \\frac{x+2}{2{sin}^{2}⁡x-3} & <(2{sin}^{2}⁡x-3)\\cdot \\frac{4-3x}{2{sin}^{2}⁡x-3} \\\\ x+2 & <4-3x \\\\ 4x & <2 \\\\ x & <\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981762/state.json" "file_sha256" "b32f01ca9cf74dcc6044655235d91ad3709a79f147029d5bf330be973fdd475e"} {"question" "q-89671" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981762/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "7994e56f5fb1194063d808625e3816e095444e38e43c82b7f99341e9ac4cb746"} {"question" "q-89671" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981762/state.json" "file_sha256" "b32f01ca9cf74dcc6044655235d91ad3709a79f147029d5bf330be973fdd475e"} {"question" "q-89671" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "x<1"] ["math" "x<4"] ["math" "x<\\frac{1}{2}"] ["math" "x>2"] ["math" "x>\\frac{3}{2}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981762/state.json" "file_sha256" "b32f01ca9cf74dcc6044655235d91ad3709a79f147029d5bf330be973fdd475e"} {"question" "q-89671" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "x<\\frac{1}{2}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981762/state.json" "file_sha256" "b32f01ca9cf74dcc6044655235d91ad3709a79f147029d5bf330be973fdd475e"} {"question" "q-89117" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Solve the inequality\n\n$$\n\\frac{3x+2}{1-x}>4,\\,x>2.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981762/state.json" "file_sha256" "b32f01ca9cf74dcc6044655235d91ad3709a79f147029d5bf330be973fdd475e"} {"question" "q-89117" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Since $x>2,$ we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & >2 \\\\ -x & <-2 \\\\ 1-x & <-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis means that $1-x$ is negative for all the values of $x$ that we are considering $(x>2).$ So, we can multiply both sides by $1-x,$ provided that we flip the sign of the inequality:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3x+2}{1-x} & >4 \\\\ (1-x)\\cdot \\frac{3x+2}{1-x} & <(1-x)\\cdot 4 \\\\ 3x+2 & <4-4x \\\\ 7x & <2 \\\\ x & <\\frac{2}{7}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, there are no numbers that satisfy both $x>2$ and $x<\\frac{2}{7}.$ So, the inequality has no solution." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981762/state.json" "file_sha256" "b32f01ca9cf74dcc6044655235d91ad3709a79f147029d5bf330be973fdd475e"} {"question" "q-89117" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981762/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "7994e56f5fb1194063d808625e3816e095444e38e43c82b7f99341e9ac4cb746"} {"question" "q-89117" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981762/state.json" "file_sha256" "b32f01ca9cf74dcc6044655235d91ad3709a79f147029d5bf330be973fdd475e"} {"question" "q-89117" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "2<x<6"] ["math" "2<x<\\frac{5}{2}"] ["math" "x>2"] ["math" "x>\\frac{7}{2}"] ["text" "No solution"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981762/state.json" "file_sha256" "b32f01ca9cf74dcc6044655235d91ad3709a79f147029d5bf330be973fdd475e"} {"question" "q-89117" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "No solution"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981762/state.json" "file_sha256" "b32f01ca9cf74dcc6044655235d91ad3709a79f147029d5bf330be973fdd475e"} {"question" "q-329163" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Solve the inequality\n\n$$\n\\frac{8-2x}{|x-1|-1}\\ge 0,\\,x>3.\n$$\n\n{{field-1}}{{field-2}}\n$x\\le $\n{{field-3}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981762/state.json" "file_sha256" "b32f01ca9cf74dcc6044655235d91ad3709a79f147029d5bf330be973fdd475e"} {"question" "q-329163" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Since $x>3,$ we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & >3 \\\\ x-1 & >2 \\\\ |x-1| & >2 \\\\ |x-1|-1 & >1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis means that $|x-1|-1$ is positive for all the values of $x$ that we are considering $(x>3).$ So, we can multiply both sides by $|x-1|-1,$ and solve the resulting inequality as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{8-2x}{|x-1|-1} & \\ge 0 \\\\ (|x-1|-1)\\cdot \\frac{8-2x}{|x-1|-1} & \\ge (|x-1|-1)\\cdot 0 \\\\ 8-2x & \\ge 0 \\\\ -2x & \\ge -8 \\\\ x & \\le 4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we had to flip the sign of the inequality again since we divided by a negative number ($-2$).\n\nTherefore, the solution to the inequality will be the numbers that satisfy both $x>3$ and $x\\le 4.$ So, the solution to our inequality is\n\n$$\n3<x\\le 4.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981762/state.json" "file_sha256" "b32f01ca9cf74dcc6044655235d91ad3709a79f147029d5bf330be973fdd475e"} {"question" "q-329163" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981762/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "7994e56f5fb1194063d808625e3816e095444e38e43c82b7f99341e9ac4cb746"} {"question" "q-329163" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981762/state.json" "file_sha256" "b32f01ca9cf74dcc6044655235d91ad3709a79f147029d5bf330be973fdd475e"} {"question" "q-329163" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "3"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981762/state.json" "file_sha256" "b32f01ca9cf74dcc6044655235d91ad3709a79f147029d5bf330be973fdd475e"} {"question" "q-329163" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981762/state.json" "file_sha256" "b32f01ca9cf74dcc6044655235d91ad3709a79f147029d5bf330be973fdd475e"} {"question" "q-329163" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "<"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981762/state.json" "file_sha256" "b32f01ca9cf74dcc6044655235d91ad3709a79f147029d5bf330be973fdd475e"} {"question" "q-329163" "field" "field-3" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981762/state.json" "file_sha256" "b32f01ca9cf74dcc6044655235d91ad3709a79f147029d5bf330be973fdd475e"} {"question" "q-329163" "field" "field-3" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "4"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981762/state.json" "file_sha256" "b32f01ca9cf74dcc6044655235d91ad3709a79f147029d5bf330be973fdd475e"} {"question" "q-116273" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Solve the inequality\n\n$$\n\\frac{x}{x-3}\\le 2,\\,x>5.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981762/state.json" "file_sha256" "b32f01ca9cf74dcc6044655235d91ad3709a79f147029d5bf330be973fdd475e"} {"question" "q-116273" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Since $x>5,$ we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & >5 \\\\ x-3 & >5-3 \\\\ x-3 & >2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis means that $x-3$ is positive for all the values of $x$ that we are considering $(x>5).$ So, we can multiply both sides by $x-3,$ and solve the resulting inequality as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x}{x-3} & \\le 2 \\\\ (x-3)\\cdot \\frac{x}{x-3} & \\le (x-3)\\cdot 2 \\\\ x & \\le 2x-6 \\\\ -x & \\le -6 \\\\ x & \\ge 6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the inequality will be the numbers that satisfy both $x>5$ and $x\\ge 6.$ So, the solution to our inequality is $x\\ge 6.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981762/state.json" "file_sha256" "b32f01ca9cf74dcc6044655235d91ad3709a79f147029d5bf330be973fdd475e"} {"question" "q-116273" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981762/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "7994e56f5fb1194063d808625e3816e095444e38e43c82b7f99341e9ac4cb746"} {"question" "q-116273" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981762/state.json" "file_sha256" "b32f01ca9cf74dcc6044655235d91ad3709a79f147029d5bf330be973fdd475e"} {"question" "q-116273" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "5<x\\le 6"] ["math" "5<x\\le 8"] ["math" "x>5"] ["math" "x\\ge 6"] ["math" "x\\ge 8"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981762/state.json" "file_sha256" "b32f01ca9cf74dcc6044655235d91ad3709a79f147029d5bf330be973fdd475e"} {"question" "q-116273" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "x\\ge 6"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981762/state.json" "file_sha256" "b32f01ca9cf74dcc6044655235d91ad3709a79f147029d5bf330be973fdd475e"} {"question" "q-89571" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Solve the inequality\n\n$$\n\\frac{2x-3}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-2)}\\ge 0,\\,x>3.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981762/state.json" "file_sha256" "b32f01ca9cf74dcc6044655235d91ad3709a79f147029d5bf330be973fdd475e"} {"question" "q-89571" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Since $x>3,$ we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & >3 \\\\ x-2 & >3-2 \\\\ x-2 & >1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis means that $\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-2)$ is positive for all the values of $x$ that we are considering $(x>3).$ So, we can multiply both sides by $\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-2),$ and solve the resulting inequality as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2x-3}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-2)} & \\ge 0 \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-2)\\cdot \\frac{2x-3}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-2)} & \\ge \\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-2)\\cdot 0 \\\\ 2x-3 & \\ge 0 \\\\ 2x & \\ge 3 \\\\ x & \\ge \\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the inequality will be the numbers that satisfy both $x>3$ and $x>\\frac{3}{2}.$ So, the solution to our inequality is $x>3.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981762/state.json" "file_sha256" "b32f01ca9cf74dcc6044655235d91ad3709a79f147029d5bf330be973fdd475e"} {"question" "q-89571" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981762/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "7994e56f5fb1194063d808625e3816e095444e38e43c82b7f99341e9ac4cb746"} {"question" "q-89571" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981762/state.json" "file_sha256" "b32f01ca9cf74dcc6044655235d91ad3709a79f147029d5bf330be973fdd475e"} {"question" "q-89571" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "3<x<4"] ["math" "3<x<\\frac{7}{2}"] ["math" "x>3"] ["math" "x\\ge 5"] ["math" "x\\ge \\frac{7}{2}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981762/state.json" "file_sha256" "b32f01ca9cf74dcc6044655235d91ad3709a79f147029d5bf330be973fdd475e"} {"question" "q-89571" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "x>3"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981762/state.json" "file_sha256" "b32f01ca9cf74dcc6044655235d91ad3709a79f147029d5bf330be973fdd475e"} {"question" "q-89572" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Solve the inequality\n\n$$\n(2x-1)(x+3)\\ge 0,\\,x<-1.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981762/state.json" "file_sha256" "b32f01ca9cf74dcc6044655235d91ad3709a79f147029d5bf330be973fdd475e"} {"question" "q-89572" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Since $x<-1,$ we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & <-1 \\\\ 2x & <-2 \\\\ 2x-1 & <-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis means that $2x-1$ is negative for all the values of $x$ that we are considering $(x<-1).$ So, we can divide both sides by $2x-1,$ provided that we flip the sign of the inequality:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(2x-1)(x+3) & \\ge 0 \\\\ \\frac{(2x-1)(x+3)}{2x-1} & \\le \\frac{0}{2x-1} \\\\ x+3 & \\le 0 \\\\ x & \\le -3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the inequality will be the numbers that satisfy both $x\\le -3$ and $x<-1.$ So, the solution to our inequality is $x\\le -3.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981762/state.json" "file_sha256" "b32f01ca9cf74dcc6044655235d91ad3709a79f147029d5bf330be973fdd475e"} {"question" "q-89572" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981762/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "7994e56f5fb1194063d808625e3816e095444e38e43c82b7f99341e9ac4cb746"} {"question" "q-89572" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981762/state.json" "file_sha256" "b32f01ca9cf74dcc6044655235d91ad3709a79f147029d5bf330be973fdd475e"} {"question" "q-89572" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-3\\le x<-1"] ["math" "-5\\le x\\le -3"] ["math" "x<-1"] ["math" "x\\le -3"] ["math" "x\\le -5"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981762/state.json" "file_sha256" "b32f01ca9cf74dcc6044655235d91ad3709a79f147029d5bf330be973fdd475e"} {"question" "q-89572" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "x\\le -3"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981762/state.json" "file_sha256" "b32f01ca9cf74dcc6044655235d91ad3709a79f147029d5bf330be973fdd475e"} {"question" "e-7901" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Solve the inequality\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{2}{5x^4 +2} \\lt \\dfrac{5-2x}{5x^4+2}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981762/example-7901.json" "file_sha256" "670d420148066481aabce859e97c0d4752e07cb8ce1336afebfd707992c2dd75"} {"question" "e-7901" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We know that the denominator $5x^4+ 2$ is positive for all values of $x.$ So, we can multiply both sides of the inequality by $5x^4+2$ and solve the resulting inequality as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{5{x}^{4}+2} & <\\frac{5-2x}{5{x}^{4}+2} \\\\ (5{x}^{4}+2)\\cdot \\frac{2}{5{x}^{4}+2} & <(5{x}^{4}+2)\\cdot \\frac{5-2x}{5{x}^{4}+2} \\\\ 2 & <5-2x \\\\ 2x & <3 \\\\ x & <\\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981762/example-7901.json" "file_sha256" "670d420148066481aabce859e97c0d4752e07cb8ce1336afebfd707992c2dd75"} {"question" "e-7902" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Solve the inequality\n\n$$\n6x^3 + 3x \\lt 0.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981762/example-7902.json" "file_sha256" "033aa455195115a6fc29dbde28716cc957fbb843974c49b56635ba8f1833d037"} {"question" "e-7902" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we factor:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6{x}^{3}+3x & <0. \\\\ 3x(2{x}^{2}+1) & <0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $2x^2 + 1$ is always positive, we can divide both sides of the inequality by this quantity. By doing this, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3x(2{x}^{2}+1)}{2{x}^{2}+1} & <\\frac{0}{2{x}^{2}+1} \\\\ 3x & <0 \\\\ x & <0.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981762/example-7902.json" "file_sha256" "033aa455195115a6fc29dbde28716cc957fbb843974c49b56635ba8f1833d037"} {"question" "e-7903" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Solve the inequality\n\n$$\n(2\\sin{x}-9)(2x) \\leq 0.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981762/example-7903.json" "file_sha256" "fc920db1fc4edad957c45a7cb6d7646b4119e7d131d1919fb6da7f082db0fc92"} {"question" "e-7903" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Note that the factor $2\\sin{x}-9$ is always negative. We know this because\n\n$$\n-1 \\leq \\sin x \\leq 1 \\quad \\Rightarrow \\quad -11 \\leq 2\\sin x - 9 \\leq -7.\n$$\n\nBecause $2\\sin{x}-9$ is always negative, we can divide both sides of the inequality by this quantity, but we have to remember to flip the sign of the inequality. By doing this, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-9)(2x) & \\le 0 \\\\ \\frac{(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-9)(2x)}{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-9} & \\ge \\frac{0}{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-9} \\\\ 2x & \\ge 0 \\\\ x & \\ge 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981762/example-7903.json" "file_sha256" "fc920db1fc4edad957c45a7cb6d7646b4119e7d131d1919fb6da7f082db0fc92"} {"question" "e-7904" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Solve the inequality\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{x+5}{1-3x} \\lt 2, \\quad x \\lt 0.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981762/example-7904.json" "file_sha256" "e02854fdee15071489a23d7594b26a96ded565045b062922650db6a87b4cc2f3"} {"question" "e-7904" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Since $x\\lt 0,$ we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & <0 \\\\ -x & >0 \\\\ -3x & >0 \\\\ 1-3x & >1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis means that $1-3x$ is positive for all the values of $x$ that we are considering $(x\\lt 0).$ So, we can multiply both sides by $1-3x,$ and solve the resulting inequality as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x+5}{1-3x} & <2 \\\\ (1-3x)\\cdot \\frac{x+5}{1-3x} & <(1-3x)\\cdot 2 \\\\ x+5 & <2-6x \\\\ 7x & <-3 \\\\ x & <-\\frac{3}{7}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the inequality will be the numbers that satisfy both $x \\lt 0$ and $x \\lt -\\dfrac{3}{7}.$ So, the solution to our inequality is $x \\lt -\\dfrac{3}{7}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981762/example-7904.json" "file_sha256" "e02854fdee15071489a23d7594b26a96ded565045b062922650db6a87b4cc2f3"}] "usage" {"q-89117" [] "e-7901" [] "q-89571" [] "q-89658" [] "e-7903" [] "e-7904" [] "e-7902" []} "basis" 1088 "retractions" [[17592186157923 :question/problem "Solve the inequality\n\n$4xe^{2x} - 5e^{2x} \\le 0$."] [17592186157914 :question/problem "Solve the inequality\n\n$\\frac{3x + 2}{1 - x} > 4, x > 2$."] [17592186157922 :question/problem "Solve the inequality\n\n$(2x - 3)/(\\ln (x - 2)) \\ge 0, x > 3$."] [17592186157109 :question/problem "Solve the inequality\n$\\dfrac{2}{5x^4 +2} \\lt \\dfrac{5-2x}{5x^4+2}$."] [17592186157109 :question/worked-solution "We know that the denominator $5x^4+ 2$ is positive for all values of $x$. So, we can multiply both sides of the inequality by $5x^4+2$ and solve the resulting inequality as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{2}{5x^{4} + 2} & < \\frac{5 - 2x}{5x^{4} + 2} \\\\ (5x^{4} + 2) \\cdot \\frac{2}{5x^{4} + 2} & < (5x^{4} + 2) \\cdot \\frac{5 - 2x}{5x^{4} + 2} \\\\ 2 & < 5 - 2x \\\\ 2x & < 3 \\\\ x & < \\frac{3}{2}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"] [17592186157110 :question/problem "Solve the inequality\n$6x^3 + 3x \\lt 0$."] [17592186157110 :question/worked-solution "First, we factor:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}6x^{3} + 3x & < 0. \\\\ 3x(2x^{2} + 1) & < 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nSince $2x^2 + 1$ is always positive, we can divide both sides of the inequality by this quantity. By doing this, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}(3x(2x^{2} + 1))/(2x^{2} + 1) & < \\frac{0}{2x^{2} + 1} \\\\ 3x & < 0 \\\\ x & < 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"] [17592186157111 :question/problem "Solve the inequality\n$(2\\sin{x}-9)(2x) \\leq 0$."] [17592186157111 :question/worked-solution "Note that the factor $2\\sin{x}-9$ is always negative. We know this because\n\n$$\n-1 \\leq \\sin x \\leq 1 \\quad \\Rightarrow \\quad -11 \\leq 2\\sin x - 9 \\leq -7\n$$\n\nBecause $2\\sin{x}-9$ is always negative, we can divide both sides of the inequality by this quantity, but we have to remember to flip the sign of the inequality. By doing this, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}(2\\sin x - 9)(2x) & \\le 0 \\\\ ((2\\sin x - 9)(2x))/(2\\sin x - 9) & \\ge \\frac{0}{2\\sin x - 9} \\\\ 2x & \\ge 0 \\\\ x & \\ge 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"] [17592186157112 :question/problem "Solve the inequality\n\n$\\dfrac{x+5}{1-3x} \\lt 2, \\quad x \\lt 0$."] [17592186157112 :question/worked-solution "Since $x\\lt 0$, we have that\n$\\begin{bmatrix}x & < 0 \\\\ -x & > 0 \\\\ -3x & > 0 \\\\ 1 - 3x & > 1\\end{bmatrix}$.\n\nThis means that $1-3x$ is positive for all the values of $x$ that we are considering $(x\\lt 0)$. So, we can multiply both sides by $1-3x$, and solve the resulting inequality as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{x + 5}{1 - 3x} & < 2 \\\\ (1 - 3x) \\cdot \\frac{x + 5}{1 - 3x} & < (1 - 3x) \\cdot 2 \\\\ x + 5 & < 2 - 6x \\\\ 7x & < - 3 \\\\ x & < - \\frac{3}{7}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the inequality will be the numbers that satisfy both $x \\lt 0$ and\n\n$$\nx \\lt -\\dfrac{3}{7}\n$$\n\nSo, the solution to our inequality is\n\n$$\nx \\lt -\\dfrac{3}{7}\n$$"]] "immutable_answers" [17592186156737 17592186156738 17592186156739 17592186156740 17592186156741 17592186156785 17592186156786 17592186156787 17592186156788 17592186156789 17592186156791 17592186156792 17592186156793 17592186156794 17592186156795] "immutable_fields" [[17592186156736 :answer-field/correct] [17592186156736 :answer-field/id] [17592186156736 :answer-field/key] [17592186156736 :answer-field/type] [17592186156784 :answer-field/correct] [17592186156784 :answer-field/id] [17592186156784 :answer-field/key] [17592186156784 :answer-field/type] [17592186156790 :answer-field/correct] [17592186156790 :answer-field/id] [17592186156790 :answer-field/key] [17592186156790 :answer-field/type]] "no_history" []}
